            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Turns a full complex spectrum, from np.fft.fft, back into a signal: the inverse Fourier transform. Each frequency's wave is rebuilt at its magnitude and phase and all of them are added.\n\nRound trip it gives back the original. The point is to change the spectrum first:\nremove or boost frequencies, shift phases, or keep only the positive frequencies of a real signal to make its \"analytic\" version, whose np.abs is a smooth envelope.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.fft.ifft\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nspectrum:\nA complex spectrum, one entry per sample, as np.fft.fft makes it. Every input is transformed and sent.\n\naxis (int box, also an inlet):\nFor a 2-D spectrum, which way the frequencies run: -1 along each row, 0 down each column.\n\nnorm:\nAn option, under the node: must match the norm used for np.fft.fft.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nsignal:\nThe rebuilt signal. When its imaginary part is only rounding (as it is for any spectrum that came from a real signal, unchanged or changed symmetrically) it is sent as plain real numbers; otherwise as complex numbers.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe wave goes to its full spectrum and straight back: the right-hand plot is the same wave, as plain numbers. Put any change to the spectrum between the two nodes to hear or see what it does.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.fft.fft makes the spectrum. np.fft.irfft is the inverse for np.fft.rfft's half spectrum. np.real takes the real part if a changed spectrum leaves a complex signal.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1150,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Turns a full complex spectrum, from np.fft.fft, back into a signal: the inverse Fourier transform. Each frequency's wave is rebuilt at its magnitude and phase and all of them are added.\n\nRound trip it gives back the original. The point is to change the spectrum first:\nremove or boost frequencies, shift phases, or keep only the positive frequencies of a real signal to make its \"analytic\" version, whose np.abs is a smooth envelope.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.fft.ifft\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nspectrum:\nA complex spectrum, one entry per sample, as np.fft.fft makes it. Every input is transformed and sent.\n\naxis (int box, also an inlet):\nFor a 2-D spectrum, which way the frequencies run: -1 along each row, 0 down each column.\n\nnorm:\nAn option, under the node: must match the norm used for np.fft.fft.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nsignal:\nThe rebuilt signal. When its imaginary part is only rounding (as it is for any spectrum that came from a real signal, unchanged or changed symmetrically) it is sent as plain real numbers; otherwise as complex numbers.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe wave goes to its full spectrum and straight back: the right-hand plot is the same wave, as plain numbers. Put any change to the spectrum between the two nodes to hear or see what it does.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.fft.fft makes the spectrum. np.fft.irfft is the inverse for np.fft.rfft's half spectrum. np.real takes the real part if a changed spectrum leaves a complex signal."
        },
